(VAR-34: reusing an already-declared name as a `For each ... in <list>` loop
 variable is claimed to reject a conflicting type. It does not. `counted` is
 declared a number, the loop rebinds it to the text elements of a list
 literal whose elements are uniformly text, and the program compiles. After
 the loop `counted` still reports Number (static) while holding a text
 pointer, so arithmetic on it yields a raw address — 4198543 on the build
 that recorded this. That exact number is not stable across builds, so the
 probe asserts the shape instead of the value. This is the defect class
 LANGUAGE.md:603-606 says the rule closes — see Discrepancy 2.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox VAR-34.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   alpha
   beta
   Number (static)
   counted holds an address, not a number
)
a number called counted is 5.
a list called words is ["alpha", "beta"].
For each counted in words,
  Print counted.

Print counted's type.
a number called total is counted add 1.
If total is greater than 1000000 then, Print "counted holds an address, not a number".
